{"reference":"Ruth 2","book":"Ruth","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Gleaning and Protection","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Gleaning is presented as survival labor: Ruth follows the harvesters to gather grain. Boaz does more than allow this. He tells her to remain in his fields, directs his workers not to harm her, gives her access to water and food, and orders extra grain to be left for her. His provision therefore includes both food and protection.","verse_refs":["Ruth 2:1-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_002_3f6bff9f75dd4efd84514c5f"]}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Ruth the Moabite","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter continues to identify Ruth as a Moabite even as she lives with Naomi and seeks help among Naomi's people. Some readers connect the book to later debates about marriage with foreigners, but whether Ruth was written as a direct response to a particular later policy remains disputed.","verse_refs":["Ruth 2:1-23","Ruth 2:2"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_002_d9f1b50c62f9c842b1308ca9"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"God's Work Through Human Action","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The book attributes food and conception to the LORD, while its events usually move through the actions of Naomi, Ruth, Boaz, workers, elders, and the women of the community. This helps readers notice that divine provision in Ruth is described alongside ordinary human decisions and generosity, rather than apart from them.","verse_refs":["Ruth 2:1-23","Ruth 2:2"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_002_4e1d01fa64bebd4791a58b49"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Larger Story","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Ruth 2's harvest scene belongs to a larger movement in the book: famine, bereavement, and Naomi's sense of emptiness give way to harvest, kinship redemption, birth, communal blessing, and genealogy. The field's provision is therefore an important turn in a story that moves from loss toward restored life and belonging.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1","Ruth 3:1-18","Ruth 4:1-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_002_9f91e9f0c8ae3a919f12facd"]},{"id":"block_5","text":"Later biblical discussion of Ammonites and Moabites includes an assembly exclusion associated with Deuteronomy 23, applied in Nehemiah 13 in connection with Tobiah's access to the temple. Read alongside Ruth, that discussion should not be treated as a simple universal ethnic ban; Ruth's story and passages welcoming faithful foreigners also belong in the larger canonical picture.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1:1-22","Ruth 3:1-18","Ruth 4:1-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_002_55f682c6368b4cb321cf425a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}